winebus: which descriptor the guest was given is not which radio the bytes leave by.

ext->desc.bus_type answers a question this file asks in two different senses,
and three places asked the second and got the first answer.

It says WHICH DESCRIPTOR THE GUEST WAS GIVEN. get_compatible_ids wants that, and
so does mgvf-0013's input-report naming: both are about what the client sees,
and both are right to ask it. It does NOT say which transport a packet leaves
by, because mgvf-0005 sets it to BUS_TYPE_USB for a pad that is physically on
Bluetooth -- that is the whole of what mgvf-0005 does.

THE DEFECT. With UsbEmulation and XInputRumble both set -- a pair this project's
launcher offers together, per title, in one panel -- mgvf-0010's haptics thread
concluded "cable" for a pad on the air. It applied the gain itself instead of
leaving it to the rewrite downstream, and then wrote a raw report 0x02 to a pad
whose Bluetooth descriptor declares no such report. Every write was refused.
XInput rumble did nothing at all, silently, for as long as that pair was set,
and the announcement at device arrival said the gain was being applied while
nothing was reaching the motors.

dualsense_speaks_bluetooth is the question those three sites meant to ask.
HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB is set only by dualsense_usb_emulation_fixups, which
returns early for anything that did not arrive over Bluetooth, and
hidraw_disable_report_fixups deliberately never clears it -- so the flag means
"physically on Bluetooth and being presented otherwise", exactly. The two sites
that legitimately mean "which descriptor" are left reading bus_type, because
there they are correct.

AND IT IS WHAT MAKES A DEFAULT POSSIBLE. mgvf-0018 added a deadband and left it
off, because the right value depends on the transport and the transport could
not be asked. It can now: zero on a cable, where a write costs 1 to 2 ms and
there is nothing to ration, and four over Bluetooth, where the link carries
about 65 reports a second and 76% of the packets this driver was sending moved a
motor by less than four steps of 255.

Measured on Beast of Reincarnation over Bluetooth: 3 to 5 frames a second and
visible stutter became 2 frames and none, with the rumble unchanged to the hand
that had been complaining about it. A registry value still overrides it, and 0
still means off.

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED. That four is right for every pad, every link and every
title -- it is one hand on one Edge on one Mac, and it is a default rather than
a constant for that reason. And the two frames that remain are not explained:
the cost is the write (mgvf-0017 proved that by removing only the write), but
why a write that burns no CPU, starves no input and provokes no mode change
costs a frame at all is still unknown, and every mechanism proposed for it so
far has been measured and eliminated.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
